# Lab 4.3 — Linux Log Analysis

## 🎯 Objective

Examine Linux system and authentication logs and understand how security events are recorded.

---

# Part A — Display Linux Machine Logs

## Step 1 — List the Log Directory

On Kali:

```bash
ls -lah /var/log
```

---

## Step 2 — View Recent Journal Entries

```bash
sudo journalctl -n 20
```

---

## Step 3 — View SSH-Related Logs

```bash
sudo journalctl _COMM=sshd -n 20
```

This displays recent journal entries associated with the SSH daemon.

---

# Part B — Authentication Logs on Metasploitable 2

## Step 1 — List Log Files

```bash
ls -lah /var/log
```

---

## Step 2 — Examine auth.log

```bash
ls -lah /var/log/auth.log
```

---

## Step 3 — View Recent Authentication Events

```bash
tail -20 /var/log/auth.log
```

---

## Step 4 — Generate a Login Event

Switch to the laboratory account:

```bash
su msfadmin
```

Enter the account's laboratory password.

Then inspect the log:

```bash
tail -20 /var/log/auth.log
```

---

## Step 5 — Search for Failed Authentication

```bash
grep -i "failed" /var/log/auth.log | tail -10
```

This can help identify failed authentication attempts.

---

## Step 6 — Demonstrate Log Truncation

> ⚠️ Perform this only on the isolated Metasploitable 2 laboratory VM.

```bash
sudo truncate -s 0 /var/log/auth.log
```

---

## Step 7 — Verify the File

```bash
ls -lah /var/log/auth.log
```

Then:

```bash
tail /var/log/auth.log
```

The file has been truncated.

---

## 🧹 Cleanup

Because this demonstration intentionally modifies a system log, the preferred cleanup method is:

```text
Restore the Metasploitable 2 VM from its clean VMware snapshot.
```

Do not perform destructive log manipulation on production systems.

---

## 🧠 What You Learned

Linux logs are important sources of forensic information.

Authentication logs can help identify:

* Successful logins
* Failed authentication
* SSH activity
* User switching
* Suspicious authentication patterns

Centralized logging and log integrity controls help protect this evidence.
